WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.888
Welcome to Mount Rushmore! My name is George Washington.

2
00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:09.687
I'm Thomas Jefferson. My name is Abraham Lincoln.

3
00:00:09.688 --> 00:00:15.460
And I'm Theodore Roosevelt. We are the presidents of Mount Rushmore.

4
00:00:15.461 --> 00:00:21.767
Over fifty years ago, our faces were carved into these rocks here in the western state of South Dakota.

5
00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:24.301
You may be asking how the four of us got here.

6
00:00:24.302 --> 00:00:30.917
Well, that is a long story that began in December of 1923 with a man named Doane Robinson.

7
00:00:30.918 --> 00:00:34.590
Doane Robinson was a true lover of American history.

8
00:00:34.591 --> 00:00:42.372
His dream was to capture western American history in the rocks of South Dakota in a way that had never been done before.

9
00:00:42.373 --> 00:00:46.292
He wanted to create a memorial that would make South Dakota famous.

10
00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:55.401
You see, before our faces were put here, many people had never even heard of South Dakota, let alone come here for a vacation.

11
00:00:55.402 --> 00:01:02.146
But Robinson believed that people would love the mountains of South Dakota if there were something exciting to look at.

12
00:01:02.147 --> 00:01:06.368
Robinson talked to everyone he knew and told them about his great plan.

13
00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:12.251
Of course, some people laughed at him and said that his plan was foolish, but many others listened to him.

14
00:01:12.252 --> 00:01:18.235
They agreed that carving the faces of famous Americans would be a great historical monument for the United States

15
00:01:18.236 --> 00:01:21.856
and would bring many tourists to their small state.

16
00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:27.996
Within a few months, Robinson had convinced many important men, and they decided to start the project.

17
00:01:27.997 --> 00:01:31.561
Robinson found the only man in America that could do the job.

18
00:01:31.562 --> 00:01:38.139
His name was Gutzon Borglum. He was the most famous American artist and sculptor at that time.

19
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:44.460
When Gutzon heard about this project, he was very excited and boarded the first train for South Dakota.

20
00:01:44.461 --> 00:01:54.294
Robinson was thinking about carving the faces of Western heroes, such as General George Armstrong Custer, Buffalo Bill Cody, or Lewis and Clark.

21
00:01:54.295 --> 00:01:56.850
However, when Gutzon Borglum arrived,

22
00:01:56.851 --> 00:02:03.767
he told Robinson that he wished to carve the faces of more remarkable Americans that would illustrate the history of our country.

23
00:02:03.768 --> 00:02:07.681
Naturally, he was thinking about the four of us.

24
00:02:07.682 --> 00:02:13.656
There are many mountains in South Dakota, but most of them were not suitable to carve our faces into.

25
00:02:13.657 --> 00:02:17.919
On some mountains, the rock was too soft or it had too many cracks in it.

26
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:27.001
In other places, there was not enough sunlight, so our faces would always be in shadow. Gutzon searched for the perfect location.

27
00:02:27.002 --> 00:02:31.893
Then one day Gutzon came to a tall cliff that was surrounded by pine trees.

28
00:02:31.894 --> 00:02:36.833
The name of the mountain was Mount Rushmore, near the little town of Keystone.

29
00:02:36.834 --> 00:02:40.957
The rock was flat and hard, and it had the perfect amount of sunlight.

30
00:02:40.958 --> 00:02:48.633
"Here is the place!" Gutzon cried. "I will carve America's history here."

31
00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:54.423
It took Gutzon Borglum and 400 men almost 14 years to carve our faces into these rocks.

32
00:02:54.424 --> 00:03:02.645
The workmen were miners who had spent their lives smashing rocks, so at first many of them considered it to be a normal job.

33
00:03:02.646 --> 00:03:07.998
Since they were poor, they were happy to earn money to buy food for their families.

34
00:03:07.999 --> 00:03:13.030
But as our faces began to appear, they became much more excited about their work.

35
00:03:13.031 --> 00:03:18.278
They were not simply making money; they knew they were building American history.

36
00:03:18.279 --> 00:03:22.989
Norman Anderson was one of the carvers that helped make our faces look true to life.

37
00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:32.536
He was proud to say, "I put the curl in Lincoln's beard, the part in Teddy's hair, and the twinkle in Washington's eye."

38
00:03:32.537 --> 00:03:36.225
But how did these workmen make us look so lifelike?

39
00:03:36.226 --> 00:03:41.600
First, Gutzon made a statue of each of us, which he brought to the construction site.

40
00:03:41.601 --> 00:03:47.877
Then, using special measurement instruments, the workmen made an exact copy of the statues in the rock.

41
00:03:47.878 --> 00:03:53.485
The only difference was that the rock carving was 12 times bigger than the statues.

42
00:03:53.486 --> 00:03:59.085
A protractor was placed at the top of the statues' heads and a bigger one near the top of the mountain.

43
00:03:59.086 --> 00:04:00.945
To make Lincoln's eye, for example,

44
00:04:00.946 --> 00:04:09.493
the workers would use a string attached to the protractor on the statue to measure the distance from the top of his head to the top of his eye.

45
00:04:09.494 --> 00:04:15.191
Then they would use a string attached to the protractor on the mountain that was 12 times longer.

46
00:04:15.192 --> 00:04:21.324
Gutzon Borglum died just before our faces were completed, so he never got to see his final masterpiece.

47
00:04:21.325 --> 00:04:35.244
But his son, Lincoln Borglum, and the workmen spent one more year finishing this great piece of American history, which is now called Mount Rushmore.

